Subject: Findings from UAT C&B Desktop Performance Analysis – IER Enterprise Records Plugin

Hi Team,

Leo and I had a working session today to investigate the desktop loading delay observed in the C&B UAT desktop. Below are our findings.

Findings
The C&B desktop currently has the following custom features enabled:
Search
Document Upload
Work Item Search
Custom Search
EDS Plugin
Other required custom plugins
Initially, all deployed plugins (including IBM Enterprise Records (IER)) were enabled for the desktop. With this configuration, the desktop consistently took 10–12 seconds to load.
To isolate the issue, we disabled all plugins and then enabled them one by one while measuring the load time.
Observation:
With all custom plugins enabled except IBM Enterprise Records (IER), the desktop loaded consistently within 2–3 seconds.
As soon as the IBM Enterprise Records (IER) plugin was enabled, the desktop load time increased back to 10–12 seconds.
We observed the same behavior in both:
The new product/UAT environment.
The current UAT environment.
Additional Verification
We checked the current Production (KWCN) desktop and noticed that the IBM Enterprise Records (IER) plugin is already disabled there, indicating that it is currently not being used in Production.
We also performed functional validation after disabling the IER plugin:
Desktop loaded successfully.
Search functionality worked as expected.
Right-click Delete operation worked correctly.
Delete reason prompt appeared as expected.
Documents were deleted successfully.
We did not observe any functional issues during our testing.
Question / Request

Could everyone please verify whether any of your custom plugins or functionality has a dependency on the IBM Enterprise Records (IER) plugin?

If there are no dependencies, we propose disabling the IER Enterprise Records plugin on the C&B desktop. This improves the desktop startup time significantly—from approximately 10–12 seconds down to 2–3 seconds.

Please let us know if you foresee any impact or have any concerns before we proceed.
